INSERT INTO `listening_passages` VALUES (1,'general','Planning a Weekend Picnic','A1','Hi Anna. Are you free this Saturday? I want to go to the park for a picnic. The weather should be nice and sunny. We can meet at the main gate at ten o\'clock in the morning. I plan to bring some fresh fruit, sandwiches, and juice for us to drink. Do you want to bring anything else? Maybe some chocolate cookies or a blanket to sit on? Let me know if you can come. Text me back tonight.','2026-09-17 00:40:09','2026-09-17 00:40:09'),(2,'general','Planning a Birthday Party','A1','Sarah: Hi Mark. Are you free this Saturday? I am having a small birthday party at my house. Mark: Oh, hi Sarah. Yes, I am free this Saturday. Happy early birthday! Sarah: Thank you. The party starts at half past six in the evening. We are going to order some pizza and buy a big chocolate cake. Mark: That sounds great. Can I bring anything with me? Sarah: You can bring some drinks like cola or orange juice if you want. Mark: Sure, I will bring some orange juice. Do I need to bring my guitar too? Sarah: Oh yes, please bring your guitar. We can play music and sing songs together. Mark: Perfect. See you on Saturday at half past six. Sarah: See you then. Bye.','2026-09-17 00:40:17','2026-09-17 00:40:17'),(4,'general','Planning a Weekend Trip','A2','Sarah: Hi Mark! Are you free this weekend? I want to go somewhere nice. Mark: Hey Sarah! Yes, I am totally free. Where do you want to go? Sarah: Well, I was thinking about visiting the local botanical garden on Saturday morning. They have a special orchid exhibition right now. Mark: That sounds lovely. I love flowers. What time should we meet? Sarah: Let us meet at nine thirty near the main entrance. Is that okay for you? Mark: Yes, nine thirty is perfect. Should we bring some snacks or lunch? Sarah: We can buy some food at the garden cafe. They sell great sandwiches and coffee. Mark: Awesome. I will bring my camera to take some nice photos. See you on Saturday! Sarah: See you then, bye!','2026-09-17 00:40:48','2026-09-17 00:40:48'),(7,'general','Planning a Weekend Camping Trip','B1','Hey Mark, are you free this weekend? My friends and I are planning to go camping at Pine Valley, and we would love for you to join us. We are planning to leave on Saturday morning at eight o\'clock so we can beat the traffic and set up our tents before lunch. We will stay for one night and return on Sunday afternoon. You do not need to worry about bringing a tent because I have a spare one you can use. However, you should definitely bring a warm jacket and a good pair of hiking boots, because the weather forecast says it might get quite chilly at night and we are planning a long walk through the hills on Sunday morning. Also, could you please bring some snacks to share around the campfire? Let me know by tomorrow evening if you can make it so we can finalize the food shopping.','2026-09-17 00:41:11','2026-09-17 00:41:11'),(9,'general','Planning the Weekend Trip','B2','Well, Sarah, I have been looking at the weather forecast for our weekend trip to the mountains, and it looks like we might get some rain on Saturday morning. Oh really? That is a bit annoying because we were hoping to go hiking early. What time does the forecast say it will clear up? It should stop raining by around one thirty, so we can still have a picnic lunch and do a shorter trail in the afternoon. That sounds like a solid plan. What about accommodation? Did you manage to book that cozy cabin near the lake? Yes, I did. I called them yesterday, and they confirmed our reservation for two nights, checking in on Friday evening. Perfect. I will pack some board games just in case the weather turns bad again on Sunday. Excellent idea, and do not forget to bring your waterproof jacket.','2026-09-17 00:41:28','2026-09-17 00:41:28'),(10,'general','Planning a Community Art Project','B2','Hello everyone, and thanks for coming to the community centre meeting tonight. We are here to discuss the upcoming mural project on Elm Street. As you know, the main goal is to brighten up the neighbourhood and involve local residents of all ages. Now, regarding the timeline, we had originally planned to start painting on the first of July, but because of the predicted heavy rain, we have decided to postpone the launch by two weeks. That means we will officially begin on the fifteenth of July. We still need volunteers to help prepare the wall surface and sort out the paint supplies. If you are interested, please sign up at the reception desk before you leave tonight. We are providing free lunch for everyone who helps out during the weekend sessions. Also, remember that we are hosting a design workshop next Tuesday evening at seven o\'clock in room four. Everyone is welcome to bring their sketches and ideas.','2026-09-17 00:41:51','2026-09-17 00:41:51'),(11,'general','Discussing Urban Green Spaces','C1','Well, looking at the recent proposal for the municipal park redesign, I must say I am rather sceptical about the implementation timeline. The council expects us to finish the botanical sector by November, which strikes me as profoundly unrealistic given the current supply chain bottlenecks. We are still waiting on the specialized irrigation hardware from overseas, and without that, planting the perennial beds is out of the question. Furthermore, the budget allocation for arboriculture has been slashed by fifteen percent, meaning we will have to scale back our ambitions regarding the mature oak transplantations. That is deeply frustrating, especially since the community consultation phase overwhelmingly favoured preserving those ancient trees. If we compromise on that, we risk severe public backlash. We need to draft an urgent memo to the planning committee tomorrow morning outlining these logistical hurdles before they ratify the final blueprint at the upcoming assembly.','2026-09-17 00:41:59','2026-09-17 00:41:59'),(12,'general','Discussing the Annual Architecture Exhibition','C1','Well, having reviewed the submissions for the upcoming architecture exhibition, I must say I am rather underwhelmed by the overall standard this year. We seem to have lost that bold, experimental edge that characterized our previous showcases. Take the urban regeneration project for the docklands, for instance. While the structural engineering is undeniably sound, the aesthetic approach feels profoundly derivative. It relies too heavily on neo-classical motifs that simply do not resonate with the contemporary skyline. Furthermore, the logistical constraints imposed by the municipal council have undoubtedly stifled creativity. That being said, the proposal submitted by the graduate collective from the downtown campus offers a glimmer of hope. Their integration of vertical biophilic facades is genuinely innovative and demonstrates a sophisticated understanding of sustainable urban ecosystems. We definitely need to allocate prime gallery space to their installation. Let us reconvene on Thursday morning to finalize the catalogue layout and ensure these standout pieces receive the prominence they deserve.','2026-09-17 00:42:07','2026-09-17 00:42:07'),(13,'general','Urban Architecture Lecture','C1','Welcome back, everyone. Today we are continuing our examination of contemporary urban design. As cities expand rapidly, architects face unprecedented challenges regarding sustainability and spatial efficiency. Take the revitalization of the old docklands district, for instance. Initially, city planners proposed a complete demolition of the historical warehouses to maximize commercial footprints. However, following intense public backlash and rigorous heritage assessments, the council adopted a much more nuanced approach. They decided to integrate modern structural frameworks within the existing nineteenth-century brick facades. This hybrid model not only preserves the historical identity of the waterfront but also significantly reduces the carbon footprint associated with new construction. Furthermore, incorporating vertical gardens on the southern elevations has drastically improved local microclimates and biodiversity. As we will discuss in depth during our seminar this afternoon, this adaptive reuse strategy represents a paradigm shift in how we conceptualize metropolitan growth. Please ensure you have read the assigned case study on page forty-two before we reconvene.','2026-09-17 00:42:16','2026-09-17 00:42:16'),(14,'everyday_conversation','Booking a Table at a Restaurant',NULL,'Mark: Good morning, Cafe Bella, how can I help you? Sarah: Hi, I would like to book a table for tomorrow evening, please. Mark: Certainly. For how many people? Sarah: There will be four of us. Mark: And what time would you like? Sarah: Is seven thirty available? Mark: Let me check. Yes, we have a table at seven thirty. May I have your name, please? Sarah: Yes, it is Sarah Jenkins. Mark: Thank you, Ms Jenkins. We will see you tomorrow at seven thirty. Sarah: Thank you so much. Goodbye. Mark: Goodbye.','2026-09-17 00:42:23','2026-09-17 00:42:23'),(16,'everyday_conversation','Booking a Table at a Cafe',NULL,'Emma: Hi there, I would like to book a table for this Friday evening please. Mark: Hello! Of course, I can help you with that. How many people are coming? Emma: There will be four of us. We want to sit outside in the garden area if possible. Mark: Let me check. Yes, we have one table left outside. What time would you like to arrive? Emma: Is seven thirty okay? Mark: Seven thirty is fine. Could I have your name and phone number, please? Emma: Yes, my name is Emma Smith and my phone number is zero seven nine four two three one five eight. Mark: Great, Emma. Your table for four is booked for this Friday at seven thirty. See you then! Emma: Thank you so much, bye!','2026-09-17 00:42:38','2026-09-17 00:42:38'),(17,'social_monologue','Welcome to Riverside Community Centre',NULL,'Good morning everyone and welcome to the Riverside Community Centre. I am Sarah, the centre manager, and I am here to give you a quick orientation before your classes begin today. First of all, let me remind you about our membership cards. You must bring your card with you every time you visit, and remember to scan it at the front desk when you arrive. Next, I want to tell you about our facilities. The main cafeteria is located on the ground floor next to the reception area, and it serves hot meals until two o\'clock every afternoon. If you need to use the computer lab, it is situated on the second floor, room two oh four. Please note that food and drinks are strictly prohibited inside the computer lab. Finally, if you have lost any personal items, our lost and property office is open Monday through Friday from nine in the morning until five in the evening. If you have any questions during your time here, please feel free to ask any member of our staff. Have a wonderful day and enjoy your activities.','2026-09-17 00:42:46','2026-09-17 00:42:46'),(18,'social_monologue','Welcome to Pinecrest Community Centre',NULL,'Good morning everyone and welcome to the Pinecrest Community Centre. My name is Sarah, and I am the manager here. Today, I am going to give you a quick orientation about our facilities and weekly activities. First of all, the main reception desk is open from eight in the morning until nine at night, Monday through Saturday. If you need to register for any classes, you can do so online or directly at the desk. We offer a wide range of classes for all ages, including yoga, painting, and computer skills. Our most popular activity is the Friday evening social club, which starts at seven o\'clock in the main hall. Please remember that all members must carry their membership cards with them at all times while inside the building. If you lose your card, a replacement can be issued at the reception for a small fee of five dollars. Finally, if you are interested in volunteering for our upcoming summer festival, please sign up by next Wednesday. Thank you for listening, and please feel free to ask me any questions after this tour.','2026-09-17 00:43:09','2026-09-17 00:43:09'),(19,'academic_discussion','Planning the Environmental Science Presentation',NULL,'Emma: Hi Liam. Have you had a chance to look at the guidelines for our environmental science presentation next week? Liam: Yes Emma, I read them this morning. We need to focus on renewable energy sources, specifically solar and wind power in urban areas. Emma: Right. The instructions state that the presentation must include at least one case study. I was thinking we could use the new solar farm project in downtown Manchester. Liam: That is a brilliant idea. Manchester has made great progress with that initiative over the past two years. What about the visual aids? The professor mentioned we need a clear infographic. Emma: I can design the infographic tonight if you handle the spoken introduction. Liam: Perfect. Let us meet in the library at three thirty tomorrow to practice our timing.','2026-09-17 00:43:17','2026-09-17 00:43:17'),(22,'academic_lecture','The Evolution of Urban Green Spaces',NULL,'Good morning, everyone. Today we are examining how urban green spaces have transformed over the past two centuries. Initially, public parks were designed primarily as aesthetic sanctuaries, providing a refuge from the soot and congestion of industrial cities. However, contemporary landscape architecture emphasizes ecological functionality rather than mere ornamentation. Modern city planners integrate permeable pavement, native flora, and bioswales to mitigate stormwater runoff and urban heat island effects. Furthermore, empirical studies consistently demonstrate that access to biodiverse natural environments significantly improves cognitive restoration and psychological well-being among urban residents. Consequently, these vegetative zones are no longer viewed as optional luxuries, but rather as critical infrastructure essential for climate resilience and public health. As cities continue to densify rapidly, preserving and expanding these living networks presents a formidable challenge for municipal authorities worldwide. We must therefore reconsider traditional zoning laws to prioritize biodiversity conservation alongside residential development.','2026-09-17 00:43:41','2026-09-17 00:43:41'),(27,'general','Meeting a Friend at the Cafe','A1','Hi Anna. Oh, it is so good to see you. How are you doing? I am pretty good, thanks. Just a little busy with my new job. Wow, a new job. That is fantastic. What are you doing? I am working as a receptionist at a hotel downtown. It is nice, but the morning commute is very long. I have to wake up at six thirty every day. That is early. Does the job start at eight? Yes, exactly at eight o\'clock. But the best part is that I finish work at four in the afternoon. So I have my evenings completely free. That sounds great. Would you like to get some coffee now? We can sit outside in the garden area. I would love that. Let us go inside and order first. I want a large latte and a chocolate muffin. Perfect. I will just have a cup of green tea.','2026-09-17 00:58:03','2026-09-17 00:58:03'),(29,'general','Discussing Morning Weather and Outdoor Plans','A1','Anna: Good morning, Mark! What a gray and rainy day outside. Mark: Morning, Anna! Yes, it is pouring right now. I checked the weather app, and it says the rain will stop around one o\'clock. Anna: That is good news. We still need to buy groceries for the week. Should we go to the supermarket now or wait until the afternoon? Mark: Let us wait until the rain stops. We can take the umbrella and walk to the local market on Green Street at two o\'clock. Anna: Perfect plan. After that, we can also stop by the bakery to pick up some fresh bread and two chocolate cookies. Mark: Sounds delicious. I will put on my yellow raincoat and boots when we leave.','2026-09-17 01:07:31','2026-09-17 01:07:31'),(30,'general','Discussing the Science Class Project','A1','Hi Anna. Are you ready for our science class project about plants? Yes, Tom. I have my notebook right here. We need to finish our report by Friday afternoon. That gives us three days. Perfect. What should we do first? We need to draw a diagram of a bean plant and write down how much water it needs every day. I can draw the plant because I am good at art. Great. And I can write the information about the water. Do we need to bring any colored pens to school tomorrow? Yes, please bring red and green pens. Okay, I will put them in my school bag now. See you tomorrow morning at eight o\'clock.','2026-09-17 01:07:39','2026-09-17 01:07:39'),(31,'general','Taking Care of Max the Dog','A2','Sarah: Hi Mark! Thanks so much for looking after my dog Max this weekend while I am away. Mark: No problem at all, Sarah! I love spending time with him. What time do I need to feed him on Saturday? Sarah: Just give him one bowl of dry food at eight in the morning, and another bowl at six in the evening. Also, make sure he always has fresh water in his bowl. Mark: Got it. Eight in the morning and six in the evening. Does he need to go for a long walk? Sarah: Well, he usually gets tired quickly, so a short walk around the park for twenty minutes is enough. Oh, and don\'t forget to brush his fur once a day because he sheds a lot right now. Mark: Perfect, I can do that easily. Where is his brush? Sarah: It is hanging on the hook right beside the back door. Call me if you need anything else!','2026-09-17 01:07:47','2026-09-17 01:07:47'),(32,'general','Cooking Dinner Together','A2','Mark: Hey, Anna! Are you ready to cook dinner tonight? I bought some fresh vegetables and chicken from the market this morning. \nAnna: Hi Mark! Yes, I am so hungry. What are we making? \nMark: I want to try a new recipe for chicken curry. Do you like spicy food, or should we keep it mild? \nAnna: I love spicy food! But can we add some coconut milk to make it creamy? \nMark: Definitely. I also picked up four red chillies and some fresh garlic to give it a really great flavor. \nAnna: Perfect. What time will it be ready? I have an online meeting at seven o\'clock. \nMark: Don\'t worry, we started early. It will be ready by half past six. \nAnna: Great. Let me wash my hands and help you chop the onions.','2026-09-17 01:07:55','2026-09-17 01:07:55'),(33,'general','A Weekend Trip to Da Nang','B1','Hi Linh! Oh, I am so glad to see you. How was your trip to Da Nang last weekend? It was honestly amazing, Minh. I went with my cousin, and we stayed for three days. We arrived on Friday afternoon and checked into a small hotel right near My Khe Beach. The weather was a bit cloudy at first, but thankfully it did not rain at all. On Saturday morning, we rented a motorbike and drove up to the Marble Mountains. The view from the top was breathtaking, though climbing all those stone steps made us really tired. In the afternoon, we visited the Han Market to buy some souvenirs and local snacks. I got some dried mangoes and a lovely green hat. Sunday was mostly relaxing by the ocean and trying out some local seafood at a nice restaurant called Seafood Corner. We had grilled squid and spicy shrimp, which cost about four hundred thousand dong in total. I definitely want to go back there again soon.','2026-09-17 01:08:03','2026-09-17 01:08:03'),(34,'general','A Phone Call About Saturday Café Plans','B1','Hey Mark, it is Sarah. I was just wondering if you wanted to meet up this Saturday. I know we talked about going to the new art gallery downtown, but the weather forecast says it is going to rain all morning. Instead, I was thinking we could grab a coffee at the Bean Corner café around two o\'clock. We could also try out that new board game shop next door afterward. Let me know if that works for you, or if you prefer Sunday instead. Call me back when you get a chance.','2026-09-17 01:08:10','2026-09-17 01:08:10'),(35,'general','Preparing the Annual Science Fair','B2','Hi Mark. Have you had a chance to look over the schedule for the upcoming science fair? We need to make sure everything is finalized by Friday afternoon. I spoke with Doctor Evans this morning, and she confirmed that the main auditorium is booked for the entire weekend. That gives us plenty of space for all twenty five project tables. However, we still need to figure out the lighting situation in the back corner. The current bulbs are far too dim for the robotics demonstrations. I was thinking we could borrow some portable spotlights from the drama department. Do you think you could ask Sarah about that during your lunch break? Also, remember to bring the printed registration forms so we can hand them out at the entrance. Let me know if you need any help with the layout design.','2026-09-17 01:08:18','2026-09-17 01:08:18'),(36,'general','Morning Weather and Grocery Plans','B2','Mark: Morning, Sarah! Can you believe this pouring rain outside? It completely ruined my morning run. Sarah: Oh, I know, Mark. The forecast said it would clear up by noon though, so we still have hope. Mark: Well, I really hope so because I need to cycle over to the central market to pick up some fresh vegetables and organic beef for tonight\'s dinner party. Sarah: Oh, are you still hosting that dinner for seven people today? Mark: Yes, exactly. Seven guests are coming over at seven thirty this evening. Sarah: Wow, that is a full house. You had better wait until the rain stops around twelve thirty before heading out, otherwise you will get soaked. Mark: Good point. I will use the delay to tidy up the living room and prep the dessert. By the way, do you want to join us tonight? Sarah: I would love to, but I promised my sister I would help her paint her new apartment this afternoon. Maybe next time! Mark: No worries at all. Enjoy the painting!','2026-09-17 01:08:27','2026-09-17 01:08:27'),(37,'general','A Day in the Life of a Senior Conservator','C1','My alarm usually goes off at quarter to six, though I rarely need it nowadays. After a brisk twenty minute jog through Hyde Park, I grab a flat white from the corner kiosk before arriving at the Victoria and Albert Museum by half past seven. Mornings are invariably devoted to meticulous hands on restoration work. Today, for instance, I spent three uninterrupted hours consolidating flaking paint on an eighteenth century Venetian altarpiece using a binocular microscope and a very fine sable brush. Following a brief lunch in the staff canteen, where I typically review condition reports, my afternoons shift dramatically in tempo. I generally conduct curatorial meetings, liaise with international couriers regarding incoming loans, or supervise doctoral interns handling fragile textiles. By the time I lock up my studio at six o\'clock, my eyes are usually aching, but the profound sense of stewardship makes every arduous hour worthwhile.','2026-09-17 01:08:35','2026-09-17 01:08:35'),(38,'general','Caring for Barnaby the Golden Retriever','C1','Liam: Morning, Sarah! Are you ready for the weekend trip? I just wanted to go over the final schedule for looking after Barnaby. Sarah: Oh, absolutely! I have got everything written down. Remind me again, what time does he need his morning medication? Liam: Well, the vet said he must have his joint supplement precisely at eight o\'clock, mixed right into his wet food. Sarah: Got it, eight o\'clock sharp with his wet food. And what about his afternoon walk? The weather forecast looks a bit unpredictable. Liam: Right, if it pours, we should skip the usual route through Kensington Park and just do a couple of brisk turns around the paved courtyard behind the apartment block to keep his paws clean. Sarah: Makes sense. I will stick to the courtyard if it rains. By the way, does he still have that weird aversion to the blue rubber ball? Liam: Oh, heavens no, he actually loves it now, though his absolute favorite toy is the squeaky plush fox that squeaks when squeezed. Just make sure you put all his toys back in the canvas basket by nine in the evening so he does not trip over them in the dark.','2026-09-17 01:08:44','2026-09-17 01:08:44'),(39,'everyday_conversation','Returning a Faulty Toaster at the Store',NULL,'Assistant: Good morning. Can I help you? Customer: Yes, hello. I bought this toaster yesterday, but it does not work. Assistant: Oh, I am sorry to hear that. Do you have your receipt with you? Customer: Yes, here it is. I paid twenty five pounds for it at branch number four. Assistant: Thank you. Let me check the system. Oh, I see the transaction. Would you like a replacement or a full refund? Customer: I would like a replacement, please. I need a toaster for my breakfast. Assistant: No problem at all. Let me grab a brand new one from the back shelf for you.','2026-09-17 01:08:54','2026-09-17 01:08:54'),(40,'everyday_conversation','Buying a Souvenir at the Weekend Market',NULL,'Emma: Excuse me, how much is this blue ceramic vase? Mark: That one is twenty five pounds, miss. It is handmade by a local artist. Emma: Oh, that is a bit too expensive for me. Would you take fifteen pounds for it? Mark: Fifteen pounds is too low, I would lose money. How about twenty pounds? That is my best price. Emma: Could you throw in that small wooden keychain as well, and I will pay twenty pounds right now? Mark: Alright, you drive a hard bargain. Twenty pounds for the vase and the keychain. Deal? Emma: Deal. Here is a twenty pound note.','2026-09-17 01:09:02','2026-09-17 01:09:02'),(41,'social_monologue','Onboard Safety Instructions for the Express Train',NULL,'Good morning passengers, and welcome aboard the Northern Express service running from Manchester to Leeds. Before we depart, please listen carefully to these important safety announcements. For your own security, please ensure that your luggage is stored securely either in the overhead racks or under your seat, and keep all aisles completely clear at all times. Smoking is strictly prohibited in all carriages, including the use of electronic cigarettes. In the unlikely event of an emergency, please locate the emergency exit doors situated at both ends of every carriage, and follow the illuminated green safety signs. Our ticket inspector will be walking through the train shortly to check your passes. We remind you that hot food and drinks are available for purchase in carriage number four. If you need any assistance during your journey, please press the red emergency call button located near the doors or speak directly to a member of our crew. Thank you for travelling with us today, and we hope you enjoy your comfortable journey to Leeds.','2026-09-17 01:09:10','2026-09-17 01:09:10'),(42,'social_monologue','Welcome to the Grand Bay Hotel',NULL,'Good afternoon, and welcome to the Grand Bay Hotel. My name is Sarah, and I am delighted to check you into room four hundred and twelve on the fourth floor. Your room rate includes our complimentary buffet breakfast, which is served every morning from six thirty until ten o\'clock in the main dining room on the ground floor. For your convenience, high speed wireless internet is available throughout the building. You can connect using the network name Grand Bay Guest, and the password is simply ocean breeze in all lowercase letters. If you plan to relax this afternoon, our outdoor swimming pool and fitness centre are located behind the main lobby and remain open until ten o\'clock tonight. Please remember to keep your room key card with you at all times, as you will need it to operate the guest elevators after eleven o\'clock in the evening. Should you require any extra pillows, a wake up call, or assistance with your luggage, just dial zero on your room telephone to reach the front desk. Thank you for choosing to stay with us, and I hope you have a wonderful visit here in coastal Vietnam.','2026-09-17 01:09:18','2026-09-17 01:09:18'),(43,'academic_discussion','Planning the Sociology Research Project',NULL,'Emma: Hi Mark. Have you looked at the guidelines for our sociology research project yet? Mark: Yes, I read through them yesterday. We need to choose our topic by Friday. Emma: Great. I was thinking we could focus on urban public spaces, specifically downtown parks. Mark: That sounds interesting. We could observe how different age groups use the community garden on Maple Street. Emma: Perfect. Let us divide the tasks. Can you handle the literature review? Mark: Sure, I can read about urban sociology theories. What about you? Emma: I will design the observation checklist and schedule our field visits. We should aim to visit the garden at least three times next week. Mark: Sounds like a solid plan. Let us meet again on Thursday afternoon to review our progress.','2026-09-17 01:09:26','2026-09-17 01:09:26'),(44,'academic_discussion','Planning the Annual Faculty Gala',NULL,'Dr. Vance: Good morning, everyone. Let us get started on planning the annual faculty gala. We need to lock down the venue, the catering, and the guest list by Friday.\nProfessor Miller: Thanks, Dr. Vance. I checked the main hall in the student center, and it is available on the fifteenth of November. It holds up to one hundred and twenty people comfortably.\nDr. Vance: That sounds like a great location. What about the catering service? Did you manage to contact Gourmet Bites?\nProfessor Miller: Yes, I did. They sent over a menu featuring local vegetarian dishes and standard buffet options. The price per head is thirty dollars, which fits nicely within our budget.\nDr. Vance: Perfect. Let us go with that menu. Now, regarding the entertainment, we should hire a live jazz band rather than a DJ this year.\nProfessor Miller: I agree completely. I know a local trio called The Blue Notes, and they usually charge four hundred dollars for a three-hour performance.\nDr. Vance: Excellent. Let us book them right away.','2026-09-17 01:09:35','2026-09-17 01:09:35'),(45,'academic_lecture','The Domestic Revolution of the Mechanical Refrigerator',NULL,'Good morning, everyone. Today we are examining the profound domestic transformation triggered by the widespread adoption of the home mechanical refrigerator in the nineteen thirties. Before this technological breakthrough, households relied heavily on the ice man, a tradesman who delivered heavy blocks of ice harvested from frozen lakes to insulated wooden cabinets. This system was notoriously unreliable, expensive, and limited by seasonal availability. The introduction of electrically powered cooling units, pioneered by companies like Frigidaire, fundamentally altered daily culinary habits and urban infrastructure. Food spoilage rates plummeted by nearly seventy percent within the first decade of widespread use. Furthermore, homemakers no longer needed to visit local markets daily to purchase perishable goods such as milk, meat, and fresh produce. Instead, families could store provisions safely for up to a week, which dramatically reduced the time spent on domestic chores and allowed more women to enter the formal workforce. The refrigerator also revolutionized nutrition, as fresh vegetables and meats retained their vitamins longer. Ultimately, this single appliance reshaped domestic architecture, prompting kitchen designers to build permanent spaces specifically dedicated to food preservation.','2026-09-17 01:09:43','2026-09-17 01:09:43'),(46,'academic_lecture','The Concept of Eternal Recurrence',NULL,'Good morning, everyone. Today we are exploring the philosophical concept of eternal recurrence, a notion most famously articulated by the German philosopher Friedrich Nietzsche in eighteen eighty two. Unlike linear models of time that dominate Western thought, eternal recurrence proposes that all existence and energy repeat an infinite number of times across infinite duration. Nietzsche first introduced this radical hypothesis in his work, The Gay Science. He asked us to imagine a demon creeping into our loneliest solitude, suggesting that our current life, with every sorrow and joy, every unedited mistake and fleeting triumph, must return down to the very same sequence. We would not be greeted with a fresh slate, but rather forced to relive the exact same trajectory eternally. This thought experiment is not merely a cosmological theory about the physics of the universe. Instead, it serves as an intense ethical test. Nietzsche called it the heaviest weight, challenging humanity to live in such a way that we could embrace every single moment without regret. If every action echoes infinitely, we are compelled to transform our daily existence into a masterpiece of self-affirmation.','2026-09-17 01:09:51','2026-09-17 01:09:51'),(47,'general','Planning Dad\'s Sixtieth Birthday Surprise','A1','Hi Anna. Are you ready to help me plan a surprise birthday party for our dad next month? He is going to be sixty years old on Saturday the twelfth of November. We need to buy a chocolate cake and some colorful balloons from the corner shop downtown. I think we should hold the party in our small garden behind the house because dad loves sitting outside in the fresh air. Let us invite twenty close family members including Uncle Mark and Aunt Sarah. I will call the bakery tomorrow morning to reserve the cake and you can send text messages to everyone on your phone. Let us make sure the food is ready by five o clock in the evening so everything goes smoothly.','2026-09-17 03:39:24','2026-09-17 03:39:24'),(48,'general','My Busy Morning Routine','A2','Hi everyone, my name is Linh, and today I want to tell you about my morning routine. I usually wake up at six o\'clock every day. First, I drink a glass of warm water and do ten minutes of stretching in my living room. Then, I take a quick shower and get dressed for work. At seven o\'clock, I walk to the small kitchen in my apartment and prepare breakfast. I always eat two fried eggs and a slice of toast with butter, and I drink a cup of black coffee. After breakfast, I brush my teeth and grab my red handbag. I leave my apartment at seven thirty to catch the number forty-five bus to my office downtown. The bus ride takes about twenty minutes, and I usually listen to my favorite English podcast during the trip. I arrive at work by eight o\'clock, ready to start my busy day.','2026-09-20 22:04:05','2026-09-20 22:04:05'),(49,'general','Babysitting the Cat','A2','Hey Linh, can you help me with something this weekend? I am going to visit my parents in Da Nang from Friday to Sunday, and I need someone to look after my cat, Luna. She is really easy to take care of. You just need to feed her twice a day, once at eight in the morning and once at six in the evening. Her food is in the kitchen cabinet, and you should give her one small can each time. Also, remember to change her water bowl every morning because she loves fresh water. One important thing is that she hates closed doors, so please keep the bedroom door open. If you can do this, I will buy you a big box of green tea ice cream when I come back on Sunday night. Let me know if you are free!','2026-09-20 22:04:05','2026-09-20 22:04:05'),(50,'general','My Busy Morning Routine','A2','Hello everyone. My name is Linh, and today I want to tell you about my morning routine during the week. My alarm clock always rings loudly at six o\'clock. I usually stay in bed for five minutes, and then I get up to brush my teeth and wash my face. At half past six, I go downstairs to the kitchen to prepare breakfast. I love drinking a warm cup of coffee and eating a bowl of beef noodle soup for my breakfast. After eating, I quickly clean the kitchen counter. By seven fifteen, I put on my uniform, grab my black leather handbag, and leave my apartment. I walk to the nearby subway station to catch the train to work. The train ride takes about twenty minutes. I always arrive at my office building before eight o\'clock so I can review my emails and start my workday peacefully.','2026-09-20 22:04:05','2026-09-20 22:04:05');
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `listening_passages` ENABLE KEYS */;
/*!40101 SET @saved_cs_client     = @@character_set_client */;
/*!40101 SET character_set_client = utf8 */;
CREATE TABLE `listening_questions` (
  `id` bigint(20) unsigned NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,
  `passage_id` bigint(20) unsigned NOT NULL,
  `type` varchar(10) NOT NULL,
  `question` text NOT NULL,
  `options` longtext CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_bin DEFAULT NULL CHECK (json_valid(`options`)),
  `correct_answer` longtext CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_bin NOT NULL CHECK (json_valid(`correct_answer`)),
  `order` int(10) unsigned NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  `created_at` timestamp NULL DEFAULT NULL,
  `updated_at` timestamp NULL DEFAULT NULL,
  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),
  KEY `listening_questions_passage_id_foreign` (`passage_id`),
  CONSTRAINT `listening_questions_passage_id_foreign` FOREIGN KEY (`passage_id`) REFERENCES `listening_passages` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE
) ENGINE=InnoDB AUTO_INCREMENT=251 DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4 COLLATE=utf8mb4_unicode_ci;
